elonging to the Order of Preachers, in 1272. Her feast day is on 2 September.", "newValue": "Ingrid of Skänninge (died 1282), a distinguished Swedish noblewoman and devout spiritual leader, was instrumental in establishing the first Dominican nunnery in Sweden, Skänninge Abbey, in 1272. Her aristocratic lineage and profound religious commitment made her a pivotal figure in the expansion of mendicant orders in Northern Europe during the high Middle Ages, offering new avenues for spiritual life and influence for women of noble birth. Her veneration, though never formally canonized by Rome, secured her status as one of Sweden’s most significant saints, locally beatified and widely revered.

Born into the powerful Östgöta magnate family, Ingrid undertook extensive pilgrimages, including journeys to Rome and potentially the Holy Land, which deepened her spiritual resolve. Upon her return, she dedicated herself to founding a community for Dominican nuns. With the patronage of King Valdemar Birgersson and later King Magnus Ladulås, Skänninge Abbey was established in 1272, becoming a vibrant center of learning and spirituality in medieval Sweden. Ingrid served as its prioress until her death on September 2, 1282, guiding the fledgling community through its formative years and embedding Dominican ideals within the Swedish ecclesiastical landscape.

